      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-342596",
        "problem": "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(2)={g}^{\u2032}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(2) & ={g}^{\u2032}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{\u2032}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
